# iterm2-perf.keys — the render-knob SSOT for this box (row 13 M8, MACHINE_CAPACITY_V2.md §11.3).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: every knob below is a ONE-TIME MACHINE TWEAK. A one-time tweak is invisible
# state — it survives in nobody's repo, it is silently reverted by an app update or a preference
# reset, and nothing notices. Declaring them here makes the intended machine state a REPO ARTIFACT
# that `scripts/iterm2-perf-parity.sh` can check for drift on a cadence.
#
# THIS FILE IS A DECLARATION, NOT AN ACTUATOR. Nothing in this repo writes these defaults. Setting
# them is the operator's C10 activation (agents are classifier-blocked from it), so until that
# activation runs, parity correctly reports DRIFT on every row. THAT IS THE EXPECTED STATE, not a
# bug — an UNSET key means "the app default is live", and the app defaults are what §11.9 measured
# as the cost.
#
# EVIDENCE: §11.9(2) — a 5-second `sample` of the live iTerm2 process. The saturated thread is
# LEGACY CPU GLYPH DRAWING (56-70% of the main thread in legacyView:drawRect: →
# iTermTextDrawingHelper), not parsing (4.7x cheaper). Metal was active for only a minority of
# sessions (93 vs 2,182 samples). Magnitudes below are from that report.
#
# FORMAT (one required knob per line, whitespace-separated):
#   <domain> <key> <type:bool|float> <expected> <why + magnitude>
# `#` comments and blank lines are ignored. Rows commented out are CANDIDATES, deliberately NOT
# parity-checked (see the block at the bottom).

com.googlecode.iterm2 disableAdaptiveFrameRateInInteractiveApps bool false THE LARGEST LEVER ~0.5-0.9 cores. Default YES EXEMPTS alternate-screen (TUI) panes from the adaptive frame-rate throttle - so our ~60 agent panes redraw at 60-120 FPS regardless of throughput. Setting false puts them back under the throttle.
com.googlecode.iterm2 maximumFrameRate float 30 Frame ceiling for the focused pane. The 30/30/10 trio together is ~0.3-0.6 cores; halving 60 FPS to 30 halves the glyph-drawing work that the measured hot thread is doing.
com.googlecode.iterm2 activeUpdateCadence float 30 Update cadence for the active session. NEW SESSIONS ONLY - existing panes keep the cadence they were created with, so the full effect lands only after panes recycle. Do not read an unchanged live number as a failed setting.
com.googlecode.iterm2 slowFrameRate float 10 Frame ceiling for non-focused panes. Cadence is visibility-gated - non-visible tabs parse but do NOT draw - so this governs the visible-but-unfocused majority, which is most of our 60 panes.
com.googlecode.iterm2 fastForegroundJobUpdates bool false ~0.15-0.4 cores - kills the SECOND-BUSIEST queue outright. Measured at 803 samples doing process-tree walking with __sysctl x387 over 1,556 procs, purely to keep each tab title's job name fresh.
com.googlecode.iterm2 animateGraphStatusBarComponents bool false Verbatim-documented WindowServer reducer. Animated status-bar graphs composite continuously; part of the ~0.1-0.3-core dimming+animation band and the one member with no ergonomic cost.
com.googlecode.iterm2 DimInactiveSplitPanes bool false Dimming forces a redraw+composite of every inactive split pane. At ~60 visible panes that is the widest blast radius of the ~0.1-0.3-core band. Ergonomic cost is real - it removes the focus cue - which is why the DimOnlyText row below is its paired middle ground.
com.googlecode.iterm2 DimOnlyText bool true The ergonomic middle ground for the row above. Where dimming still applies, dim TEXT ONLY rather than compositing a dim layer over the whole pane background - keeps a focus cue at a fraction of the draw cost.

# ── CANDIDATES — deliberately NOT parity-checked ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These are real levers with real magnitudes, held out of the required set because each needs an
# instrument-first decision that has not been made. Adding one to the required set is a one-line
# change once its measurement exists.
#
# com.googlecode.iterm2 UseMetal bool true
#     THE CEILING - Metal would move the whole 1.39-core legacy-drawing bottleneck off the CPU.
#     Held out because §11.9 grades it MEDIUM RISK at our scale: 60 panes means 60 CAMetalLayers,
#     and iTerm2 disables Metal per-tab above ~6 sessions anyway (memory
#     capability-initialized-is-not-capability-used: a loaded driver proved only SOME pane used it).
#     INSTRUMENT FIRST via showMetalFPSmeter, then decide - do not flip it blind.
# com.googlecode.iterm2 metalRedrawPeriod float 60
#     Only meaningful WITH Metal on; ships as a pair with UseMetal, never alone.
# com.googlecode.iterm2 showMetalFPSmeter bool true
#     INSTRUMENT, not a lever - the measurement that would qualify UseMetal above.
# com.googlecode.iterm2 logDrawingPerformance bool true
#     INSTRUMENT, not a lever - per-frame drawing cost to iTerm2's log; noisy, enable only during a probe.
#
# ── NOT A KNOB, recorded so it is not re-litigated ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Tab-stacking is a free structural lever nobody uses: non-visible tabs parse but do NOT draw
# (cadence is visibility-gated). Today 60/60 panes are visible. This is a HABIT change, not a
# default, so it cannot live in a parity file - but it is the cheapest render reduction available.
